# 20 MHz 802.11ax CSI data – split archives and source

This repository contains **split archives of a subset of the 20 MHz portion** of a public Wi-Fi CSI dataset. GitHub limits individual file sizes, so the 20 MHz archive (`20MHz.zip`, about 481 MB / 504,837,103 bytes) could not be uploaded as a single file and was divided into the smaller `.zip` files listed below and also the ZIP.mat and ZIP (2) exceeds 25MB limit and thus will be available after splitting the original 20MHz dataset from the link.
> **Important – these splits are NOT the complete dataset.** See [What the split files actually contain](#what-the-split-files-actually-contain). The full, authoritative data must be obtained from Zenodo (link below).

## Original dataset (please cite this, not this repository)

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Title (v1) | *Towards a Quantitative Analysis of CSI for AI/ML Based Sensing* |
| Title (v2, Oct 2025) | *802.11ax Wi-Fi Channel State Information (CSI) Measurements for Wireless Sensing* |
| Authors | Elena Tonini, Renato Lo Cigno (University of Brescia); v1 also lists Francesco Gringoli and Marco Cominelli (Politecnico di Milano) |
| Repository | Zenodo |
| Record / concept DOI | [10.5281/zenodo.14651521](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14651521) |
| Record URL | <https://zenodo.org/records/14651521> (latest version: <https://zenodo.org/records/14651521/latest>) |
| License | Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0) – <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> |
| Collection site | Department of Information Engineering, University of Brescia, Office 4, Via Branze 43, Brescia, Italy |
| Collection period (per dataset README) | 2024-07-12 to 2025-08-31 |
| Hardware / tool | Asus RT-AX82U (transmitter) and RT-AX86U / RT-AX82U (receiver), Broadcom BCM43684 chipset; CSI extracted with [Nexmon CSI](https://github.com/seemoo-lab/nexmon_csi) |
| Associated paper | E. Tonini, F. Gringoli, R. Lo Cigno, M. Cominelli, "Towards a Quantitative Analysis of CSI for AI/ML Based Sensing," *2025 IEEE Wireless Communications and Networking Conference (WCNC)*, Milan, 2025, pp. 1–6, doi: [10.1109/WCNC61545.2025.10978183](https://doi.org/10.1109/WCNC61545.2025.10978183) |

The README shipped inside the dataset (dated 2025-10-21) also refers to the version DOI `10.5281/zenodo.17225694` for the paper's experimental setups. **Please check on the Zenodo record page which exact version your copy corresponds to and cite that version DOI.** The v2 record additionally lists: E. Tonini and R. Lo Cigno, "Leveraging Mutual Information in Stochastic CSI Analysis for Wi-Fi Sensing," WONS 2026.

### Suggested citation

> E. Tonini, F. Gringoli, R. Lo Cigno and M. Cominelli, "Towards a Quantitative Analysis of CSI for AI/ML Based Sensing," 2025 IEEE Wireless Communications and Networking Conference (WCNC), Milan, Italy, 2025, pp. 1–6, doi: 10.1109/WCNC61545.2025.10978183.
> Dataset: Zenodo, doi: 10.5281/zenodo.14651521 (CC BY-SA 4.0).

We did not create this data. Everything here is redistributed under the original CC BY-SA 4.0 licence: attribution to the authors above is required, and any redistribution or derivative of the data must keep the same licence. Our own code is licensed separately (see the repository licence).

## Dataset structure (as documented by the authors)

```
20MHz/
  0people/   2024-07-12/  capture0..3 (.pcap, .mat), config.json     # empty room (E)
             2024-07-24/  capture0..3 (.pcap, .mat), config.json     # empty room (E)
  1people/   2024-07-12/  capture1..3 (.pcap, .mat), config.json     # one person sitting (S)
  4people/   2024-07-24/  capture0..3 (.pcap, .mat), config.json     # four people, meeting (M)
```

* `captureN.pcap` – raw Nexmon CSI capture; `captureN.mat` – complex CSI extracted from it with MATLAB.
* `.mat` files are **MATLAB v7.3 (HDF5)**; the variable is `capture`, stored as a real/imag compound array of shape (256, packets) in h5py, i.e. packets × 256 FFT bins in MATLAB. 242 of the 256 bins are active (802.11ax 20 MHz tones −122…−2 and +2…+122); the other 14 are exactly zero.
* `config.json` records the setup (location, `usleep` = 33 ms between frames, expected 19,000 frames, number of people, notes).
* In the 20 MHz data used by our experiments there are 15 `.mat` captures (8 empty, 3 sitting, 4 meeting).

## What the split files actually contain

The archives were produced by splitting/zipping files individually, so **the folder hierarchy was not preserved** and several parts contain only empty macOS metadata. Inspected contents:

| File | Contents |
|---|---|
| `ZIP_mat.zip` (~85 MB) | `capture0.mat`–`capture3.mat` (sizes match `0people/2024-07-12/`) |
| `ZIP__20mhz__2_.zip` (~88 MB) | `capture0.pcap`–`capture3.pcap` (sizes match `0people/2024-07-12/`) |
| `ZIP__20mhz__4_.zip` | one `config.json` (632 bytes, matches `0people/2024-07-12/config.json`) |
| `ZIP__20mhz__1_.zip` | only a macOS `._20MHz` metadata stub |
| `ZIP__20mhz__3_.zip` | only a macOS `._2024-07-24` stub |
| `ZIP__20mhz__5_.zip` | only a macOS `._2024-07-12` stub |
| `ZIP__20mhz__6_.zip` | only a macOS `._0people` stub |
| `ZIP__20mhz__7_.zip` | only a macOS `._4people` stub |
| `ZIP__20mhz__8_.zip` | only a macOS `._1people` stub |

Consequences:

1. The splits together hold only the **empty-room recordings of 2024-07-12** (4 captures) – roughly 170 MB, not the full ~481 MB archive. The empty-room 2024-07-24, one-person and four-people recordings are **not** included.
2. Files beginning with `._` are macOS "AppleDouble" resource-fork files and can be ignored/deleted.
3. After unzipping, recreate the layout manually, e.g. put the four `.mat`, four `.pcap` and the `config.json` into `20MHz/0people/2024-07-12/`.

> If you intended the repository to hold the whole 481 MB dataset, re-create the split from the original archive (see below) instead of using these files.

## Getting the complete dataset

1. Open <https://zenodo.org/records/14651521/latest> (or the DOI <https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14651521>) and download the 20 MHz archive (the record also provides 40 MHz and 80 MHz data; the v1 file `csiq_dataset.zip` is 2.1 GB).
2. Unzip it so the folder structure above is preserved.
3. Point the code at it:

```bash
export M4_DATA_ROOT=/path/to/20MHz          # Windows PowerShell: $env:M4_DATA_ROOT="C:\path\to\20MHz"
python build_inventory.py --data-root "$M4_DATA_ROOT" --out results
python run_m4_experiment.py --data-root "$M4_DATA_ROOT" --captures captures.csv --out results/real_20mhz
```

### Re-splitting a large archive for GitHub (keeps the structure)

```bash
# split into 90 MB parts (GitHub's hard limit is 100 MB per file)
split -b 90m -d 20MHz.zip 20MHz.zip.part_
# re-assemble
cat 20MHz.zip.part_* > 20MHz.zip && unzip 20MHz.zip
```
Alternatively use Git LFS, or link to Zenodo instead of re-hosting the data (preferred, because it keeps the citation and version information with the authors' record).

## Verifying integrity

Our `build_inventory.py` writes SHA-256 checksums of every `.mat` file to `results/data_checksums.json`. Compare them after reassembling to make sure no file was altered.
